\par
INTRODUCTION\par
\par
\pard This user guide, together with the documents referenced in Appendix A, provides information on the installation, maintenance and operation of the \i P?S\super\i0 [1] \nosupersub\i Enhanced PDP-8 Simulator\i0  [PEPS].\cf1   Is this really about a new PDP-8 simulator?  Should you mention SIMH in your title?  Of course, I can see how the PEPS is not just SIMH because of how & what you had to do to make everything play well together, using your arcane knowledge of Windows & everything PDP-8.\cf0\par
\par
The PEPS is designed to work on any release of Microsoft Windows XP, Windows Vista, Windows 7, Windows 8 or Windows 10.  It is generally distributed as a self-extracting archive capable of being installed on any permanent\cf1 ly-\cf0 lettered logical drive.  Due to internal restrictions, the PEPS cannot be located on network shares or removable drives where the logical drive letter could change.  Appendix B details the installation process.  Note: For illustration purposes only, the package elements will be described throughout this document as if installed on drive P:.  Any permanent\cf1 ly-\cf0 lettered logical drive device may be used; however, it is recommended the PEPS be installed on a dedicated drive to facilitate backup of working copies of the package.  Note: The use of mulitple drives in Windows is encouraged by Microsoft.  This is best accomplished using available third-party partitioning programs; however, certain Windows releases include rudimentary Administrative Tools generally capable of this task.\par
\par
Certain auxiliary programs compatible with Windows are optionally required and will be detailed in Appendix C.  These programs include various text editors and post-processing programs to facilitate viewing or printing text files created by the PEPS [such as assembly listings of user programs].  Most of the PEPS documentation files are distributed in \i Portable Document Format\i0  [PDF]. As such, a compatible document reading program [such as \i Adobe Reader\i0 ] will be required.\par
\par
The PEPS is a software implementation of a substantial configuration of the PDP-8 architecture, specifically a PDP-8/E [or similar model that uses the identical CPU modules].  Note: Additional PDP-8 CPU models are available in the package by changing configuration parameters.\cf1   Will an Appendix be added to explain these configuration parameters so that those with any of the other PDP-8 CPUs will be able to simulate running on "their" PDP-8s?\cf0\par
\par
The overall simulation is controlled by specific initialization and configuration files passed to the basic "engine" of the simulator package which is generally known as \i SIMH\i0  for the PDP-8.  The SIMH project is maintained independently and supports many computer architectures.  More information on the SIMH project can be obtained from various Internet websites.  Appendix D contains additional information specific to the PDP-8 version of SIMH that may be of interest to users of the PEPS; certain initialization parameters that are considered important options applying to the PEPS will be described in greater detail.  Note: Many of the available configuration parameters concern simulation of hardware not generally necessary to the supported single-user operating systems provided in the PEPS package; for those users seeking additional documentation, links are provided to websites that may supply additional information.  However, most users of the PEPS will not be concerned unless intending to develop software specifically targeted to these devices.\page 1.1\tab General Description\par
\par
The PEPS provides a completely functional simulation of a PDP-8 system with various peripherals.  When compared to real-world hardware of various PDP-8 systems, the configuration is considered to be a large system.  This allows much of the performance of these original systems to the extent practical, given the simulator is actually running on a Windows-based system.\par
\par
As typically configured, the following 12-bit resources are generally available within the PEPS environment:\line\line a) PDP-8/E CPU [generally known as KK8F as found in the PDP-8/E,\par
   PDP-8/F, PDP-8/M and the PDP-8/A-620 series].\par
\par
b) 32K of main memory.\par
\par
c) \i Extended Arithmetic Element\i0  [EAE].  Note: Due to the presence of\par
   the EAE, the corresponding PDP-8/A model is known as the\par
   PDP-8/A-625.\par
\par
d) Standard PDP-8 Device 03/04 console terminal with full support of\par
   DEC standard horizontal tab characters.  Advanced console terminal-\par
   handling software can depend on proper display of the console\par
   output showing horizontal tabs aligned every eight spaces, as would\par
   typically be the case on newer DEC terminals such as the VT100.\par
   However, in most respects, the console display resembles the output\par
   of a Teletype Model 33 using pale yellow roll paper and bold black\par
   ink ribbon.\par
\par
   Note: For operating systems such as OS/8\super [2]\nosupersub  with known defects in\par
   console terminal handling\cf1 ,\cf0  the console simulation can be restricted\par
   to forcing upper-case input and output, as well as suppression of\par
   unintended output of certain control characters.\par
\par
e) Device 66 standard line printer.  Note: For certain configurations\par
   this can be deliberately disabled as necessary.\par
\par
f) PC04 High-Speed Reader and Punch.  Note: Individual file transfer\par
   from the Windows host into the SIMH environment can only be\par
   achieved using the high-speed reader.  Individual file transfer\par
   from the SIMH environment to the Windows host can be accomplished\par
   using either the device 66 line printer or the high-speed punch.\par
\par
g) TC08 DECtape Controller with up to eight DECtape drive units [0-7].\par
   Note: Each logical drive must be configured by being associated\par
   with an active compatible device file that represents the contents\par
   of the DECtape mounted on the particular drive.  If the associated\par
   file doesn't exist, it will automatically be created when\par
   attempting to write to the drive.  Any non-configured drive will\par
   return a SELECT error if read access is attempted.\page h) Primary [device 77] TD8E Controller with up to two DECtape drives\par
   with the same file considerations as in g) above.  Note: The TD8E\par
   controller and the TC08 controller are mutually exclusive.\par
\par
i) RK8E Cartridge Disk Controller with up to four RK05 drives.  As in\par
   the case of DECtape drives, should the associated drive file not\par
   exist, it will automatically be created.\cf1   What "device number" is the RK8E & its drives?\cf0\par
\par
j) Primary [device 75] RX8E Controller with up to two RX01 drives.  As\par
   in the case of RK05 drives, should the associated drive file not\par
   exist, it will automatically be created.\cf1   What about the RX02?  Does this mean that SIMH's RX8E can't use RX02 images?\cf0\par
\par
Additional devices may be available depending on the specifics of the SIMH simulator in use.  Appendix D indicates available devices that may be of use; however, specific software drivers or additional utility programs may be required to achieve functional support.\par
\par
Note: A limitation of the SIMH environment is that dismountable devices such as DECtapes, RK05 and RX01 drives cannot be loaded or unloaded during SIMH sessions; a design goal of the PEPS is to allow quick exit and reentry to the next SIMH simulator session to mitigate this issue.\par
\par
1.2  General Usage\par
\par
Using the PEPS typically starts by calling up the PEPS Command directory, which serves as the "command center" for most PEPS operations.  From this \strike folder\cf1\strike0 directory\cf0 , many functions and utilities can be initiated.  This includes bringing up the SIMH PDP-8 simulator booting to one of several supported operating system configurations and various file-related functions to allow file editing and file maintenance in the host Windows environment.\par
\par
The user must have a general understanding of Microsoft Windows to make use of many of the available features of the PEPS; knowledge of the PDP-8 operating systems is essential to use the various facilities provided, as well as help to draw comparisons between the supported operating systems [if desired].  Appendix A includes references to documents for the various supported PDP-8 operating systems which can aid in their usage.\par
\par
The Command directory is described in greater detail in section 1.50 below.\par
\par
Note: The PEPS package includes desktop shortcuts to the Command directory and the underlying directory group which supports most of the implemented commands.  Users generally start in the Command directory to initiate most operations.  The underlying directory group is generally only accessed by advanced users and/or developers of the PEPS itself.  It is strongly recommended the desktop shortcut to the Command directory be installed on all systems.\page 1.3\tab Supported PDP-8 Operating Systems\par
\par
As of this writing, various configurations of two different PDP-8 operating systems are supported.\par
\par
1) OS/8 Version V3D [V3Q]\par
\par
