\pard          Figure 2.  P?S/8 Supported Resources\par
\par
Further information is provided on using these operating systems in section 1.61 below.\page 1.4\tab Using the SIMH simulator for program development\par
\par
While it is possible to execute existing PDP-8 programs within the SIMH environment, the full implementation [including the PEPS extensions] allows for meaningful development of any PDP-8 program; in many cases, work flow in the PEPS environment exceeds that of the original hardware.  Various operating modes are described below.\par
\par
1.41\tab Using the SIMH simulator for basic PDP-8 programming\par
\par
The SIMH PDP-8 simulator has been developed over a course of many years, adding in many features and functionality beyond the early implementations.  These earlier releases mimicked the most primitive PDP-8 systems which have limited capability.  Software commands were added to the simulator as a standin for a hardware front panel; at that point, the scope of what could be done was limited to what is sometimes referred to as the "paper-tape operating system" of related software programs.\line\line While this capability still remains within the SIMH simulator, the addition of a large array of storage and other peripherals allows meaningful usage of a more complex system.  Software operating systems that are booted up from the supported devices allows any level of user to accomplish much more than just a basic system [whether simulated or not].\line\line For those who only want to use this limited capability, minimal knowledge of the supported operating systems allows use of the basic software without the normally associated tedium; if the simulated environment merely consists of a CPU with memory and just a console terminal [generally a Teletype Model 33] and perhaps a PC04 high-speed reader and punch, this predictably leads to user frustration as is also the case on a real-world equivalent hardware system.\line\line Both P?S/8 and OS/8 contain commands to support the rudiments of this primitive software environment.  Appendix F details the specific progams used to support paper-tape operations available in both storage-based operating systems; only a small number of easily-learned commands are required to fully support the paper-tape environment.  While the specific programs used in the paper-tape environment are not provided with the PEPS package, they are available from various Internet-based PDP-8 archives.  Due to the nature of the overal PEPS environment, these programs may be added to allow easy deployment as required; some additional knowledge of the storage-based operating systems may be required.\page 1.42\tab Using the full PEPS environment for PDP-8 programming\line\line As compared to the paper-tape environment, the two provided operating systems [OS/8 and P?S/8] can be called up to perform far more sophisticated operations including running additional programming languages, file editors and other utilities unique to each operating system.  Each system is capable of self-contained independent operation including program development.  Commands within the PEPS Command directory are available to initiate these operations, as well as transmit files from the host Windows environment to the SIMH environment or vice-versa.\par
\par
The two operating systems can also intercommunicate the contents of files using available conversion programs, most notably the P?S/8 \i OS8CON\i0  utility for text files.  Binary files can be transferred between the two operating systems using various methods including binary paper-tape punching in one system and then binary file reading in the other.  The Command directory includes a command to swap the \i PTRIN\i0  and \i PTPOUT\i0  files for this specific purpose.  Note: Future releases of the P?S/8 operating system will include an additional utility to be known as \i BINCON\i0  patterned after OS8CON to more directly effect these transfers.  This will allow OS/8 users the ability to more readily utilize the binary output of the superior P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  program which includes features not available within OS/8's \i PAL8\i0  utility.\par
\par
Configurations of the two operating systems are each capable of booting up the other from within the same SIMH session.  Appendix G documents the available methods to transfer files between operating systems [some of which are currently under development as of this writing].  By using the best features of both systems, the PEPS can accommodate any level of usage and development possible in a PDP-8-based environment.\par
\par
1.43\tab Host editing and development of PDP-8 source files\par
\par
While the prevailing operating systems are capable of independent source file development, a distinctive feature of the PEPS environment is the support of host-level file editing with rapid transmission of updated files into the SIMH environment; multiple rounds of editing sessions and program assembly or execution [in the case of high-level languages such as FOCAL] can quickly be achieved.  While the standard Windows Notepad is the default editing program for the supplied editing commands, any properly qualifying alternative editing progam can also be used.  Appendix D contains information on some of these editing programs and certain tradeoffs involved when using them.\par
\par
In general, for any individual file-based programming project, two key icon-based commands are required that are particular to that file.  \strike a\cf1 A\cf0\strike0 s an alternative, commands are available to quickly setup any one file using generic commands.\page Note: While the process of producing a pair of file-specific custom icon-based commands can be adapted from any of the provided examples, it is recommended only advanced users attempt to create their own commands; the process requires significant knowledge of arcane features of Microsoft Windows including working with file attributes from the Windows environment [and also from a command prompt] and managing environment variables as used in the internal properties of Windows links/shortcuts.  Appendix H details the process using one of the provided file projects as an example.\par
\par
Any user of the PEPS can easily work with any number of files, changing focus between active files as necessary.  If the generic commands method is used, the only restriction is the use of one file at a time and limiting file storage to a specific directory.  This process is documented in section 1.50 below regarding commands related to the current program filename such as \i Set Current Program\i0  and \i Edit Current Program\i0 .\par
\par
As an example of a particular file-based programming project, the \i Inactive Programs\i0  directory [accessible from the main Command directory] contains a pair of commands that can be moved to the main Command directory if desired; this is accomplished using standard Windows Explorer techniques such as \i Cut\i0  or \i Copy\i0  and \i Paste\i0 :\par
\par
1) Edit PROGRAM.PAL.  This command brings up the source file\par
   \i PROGRAM.PAL\i0  for editing in the standard Windows Notepad program.\par
   Editing changes can be made at any time.  Either saving the file\par
   within the editor or exiting the editor is necessary to proceed in\par
   the development process of this file.\par
\par
Note: Appendix C contains information regarding alternate editing programs which can be substituted for Windows Notepad on an indivualized basis, as well as potential feature tradeoff considerations that may result.  These considerations apply to all file editing situations throughout the PEPS environment.  It is strongly recommended only advanced users modify the editing program parameters to call up any particular file editing situation due to potential interaction with open files and other running tasks [such as the SIMH simulator].  In certain instances, the host Windows version [32-bit or 64-bit] may also be a factor.\line\line If the editor is closed, the contents of the PROGRAM.PAL file is copied to the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file which, in turn, is used as the dedicated input to the simulated paper-tape reader device [PTR:] within the SIMH simulator environment.  Once this is accomplished any of the supported operating systems can be brought up; utilities within each operating system can then process the simulated paper-tape input followed by any desired additional utilities [such as calling up an assembler].\par
\par
2) Send PROGRAM.PAL.  This command copies the contents of the\par
   PROGRAM.PAL file to the PTRIN.TXT file as described in 1) above,\par
   then a designated operating system is brought up.  Any supported\par
   operating system can be specified for this operation; however, the\par
   same operating system is always used when executing this command.\page    Because the PROGRAM.PAL file is always copied to the PTRIN.TXT file\par
   when using the \i Send PROGRAM.PAL\i0  command, it is not necessary to\par
   close the editing session, which can stay active in a separate\par
   window.  By using this method, it is possible to achieve rapid\par
   program file development; the editing session need only be closed\par
   when done with all development operations.\par
\par
   Note: To use multiple operating systems to process the same file,\par
   it is necessary to close the editing session as described in 1)\par
   above.  At that point, opening a new SIMH session to any operating\par
   system [which does not specify any editing or other modification of\par
   the PTRIN.TXT file] will pass the last edited file contents in the\par
   PTRIN.TXT file.  This can be accomplished repeatedly since, in\par
   general, only editing sessions upon closing or send command\par
   execution will change the PTRIN.TXT file.  This can be useful when\par
   comparing operating system environments such as OS/8 and P?S/8 when\par
   passed the same file.\par
\par
The underlying implementation of these two commands are two related batch files called \i Edit and Copy PROGRAM.PAL\i0  and \i Send PROGRAM.PAL\i0 .  These files [and the shorcut\cf1 s\cf0 /links to them generally placed in the Command directory when actively used] need to be adapted to create an equivalent set of batch files and shortcut\cf1 s\cf0 /links for each user-defined file; the provided files are only meant to provide a working example.\par
\par
The user can remove all such unused components as necessary to avoid clutter when adding in new user-defined components.  Note: It is recommended \cf1 that \cf0 unneeded shortcut\cf1 s\cf0 /links be moved to the Inactive Programs directory already provided for this purpose; the underlying batch files require very little storage and might be useful for future user file development projects.\par
\par
Note: The PROGRAM.PAL file, as provided in the PEPS package, is a dummy file intended for use by those who want to quickly develop a short program in \i PDP-8 Assembly Language\i0  [PAL].  By foregoing a distinctive name, any user can quickly develop this file as far as deemed necessary.  As required, the file can later be copied to another directory with a distinctive name using Windows Explorer techniques.  \par
\par
Depending on requirements, future work can be setup in a variety of ways.  For slightly larger projects, it is recommended the user deploy the current program filename method as described above.  While this requires some additional setup effort on the part of the user, this is considered simple enough for beginning users to master after some minimal experience with the PEPS environment.\par
\par
Advanced users can create custom command pairs patterned after the method used to develop PROGRAM.PAL while applying their own unique filenames.  Using more advanced techniques, files can be located somewhere within the recommended file structure group [\i PDP-8\\Source Files\i0 ] or on any available Windows directory device as deemed appropriate.\page Appendix H documents the relevant parameters that must be set to implement any of these methods; not every project should necessarily be setup by the same general method.  Short-term projects using PROGRAM.PAL can be used used instantly while named files residing in the designated current program files directory may describe a multiple file project.  \cf1 O\cf0 nly the most advanced projects need resort to the custom named files method.\par
\par
Each method has a slightly different set of tradeoffs; as user experience grows, it will become apparent which approach serves best for any programming project.  In certain cases, working with source files created by others would contemplate little to no file editing once the proper file contents \strike is\cf1\strike0 are?\cf0  confirmed.  The custom command pairs method would generally be too tedious in this situation; copying all such files to the current program files directory would likely suffice.\par
\par
1.50\tab Operating the PEPS from the Command directory\par
\par
The Command directory shown in Figure 3 includes all commands associated with the current release.  This includes working commands provided as examples illustrating host editing of specified text files  and the transmission of updated files into the SIMH simulated environment using the supplied operating systems.  Additional commands are provided to process various files returned from the SIMH simulated environment as well as perform other functions as described below.\par
\par
The specific details of the Command window are subject to user preference considerations with regard to approaching the Windows desktop.  The commands shown in Figure 3 are configured according to certain Windows parameters and other factors currently in effect:\par
 \par
\tab a) Medium icons are in use; other available alternatives include\par
\tab    smaller and larger icons, full details and a larger tiled\par
\tab    format; some of these alternatives will fully display the\par
\tab    captions labeling all icons regardless of label character\par
\tab    count [albeit likely occupying a larger portion of the overall\par
\tab    Windows desktop].\par
\par
\tab    In this example, one of the icons was deliberately selected\par
 \tab    [thus the color is darker] for the purpose of expanding the\par
 \tab    longer caption text.  Due to the way Windows operates, when\par
\tab    not selected, the caption text will generally become truncated\par
 \tab    unless one of the other view alternatives is used.\par
\par
  \tab    In general, most of the captions are completely shown even\par
  \tab    when not selected; the medium icons and supplied captions\par
 \tab    represent a reasonable compromise of readability and clutter\par
 \tab    avoidance.\page\tab b) All command icons are positioned by asserting \i Align to Grid\par
\i0\tab    with the associated \i Auto Arrange\i0  option disabled.  Settings to\par
\tab    accomplish this are available within the window view\par
\tab    particulars available in all supported Windows versions\par
\tab    [perhaps requiring a small patch to the desktop].  Note:\par
\tab    Third-party patches for the affected systems are described in\par
\tab    Appendix I.\par
\par
\tab c) The grouping of icons in the Command window is primarily by\par
 \tab    similarity of function.  As necessary, empty positions are\par
 \tab    deliberately reserved for the purpose of future expansion.\par
 \tab    Additional commands will likely be added in future releases.\par
  \tab    Several examples of specific file editing and transmitting\par
 \tab    the updated file to the SIMH simulator are provided.  \par
 \tab    Additionally, a series of commands is provided to implement\par
\tab    generic file processing requiring no changes to the Command\par
\tab    window.\par
\par
\tab d) The general icon color scheme chosen includes specific colors\par
\tab    for related functions as well as colors associated with two of\par
\tab    the supported operating systems [OS/8 and P?S/8 configured\par
\tab    without limitations].  These color cues assist the user in\par
\tab    selecting the desired function.  Advanced users can modify any\par
\tab    of these considerations; however, beginners are advised to\par
\tab    work with the standard release as documented here.\par
\par
Note: Advanced users are encouraged to use custom icons for any command placed into the Command directory; the only requirement is \cf1 to \cf0 plac\cf1 e\cf0\strike ing\strike0  any additional icon files into the same path already used by the provided icons.  This will greatly simplify the creation of new command shortcuts/links which is complicated by the way Windows functions during the process of creating new icon-specific details within any shortcut opened for modification.  This is a severe limitation of Windows that cannot be overcome; by using the same directory for all active icon files, the path strings within the shortcut can be safely edited by changing only the trailing end of the path string [which is the icon filename] while maintaining the leading portion of the path string.  More information on creating custom shortcuts/links is available in Appendix H.\page\par
