\par
1.60\tab Description of individual commands within the Command Window\par
\par
\tab Note: The five rows of the command window shown in Figure 3\par
 \tab are described below in left to right order.\par
\par
1.61\tab Supported Operating Systems\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3, commands to launch various \tab operating systems are located in the top row of the Command\par
\tab directory window.\page\tab a) OS/8.  This is generally OS/8 Version 3D [aka Version 3Q].  In \tab    certain cases, system programs have been added taken from\par
 \tab    newer OS/8 family releases; these are known to correct bugs in \tab    the original programs.  Certain non-DEC programs are also\par
 \tab    provided to perform additional tasks, such as certain Kermit-\tab\par
 \tab    12 utilities written by Charles Lasner.  These utilities are\par
 \tab    needed to process files obtained externally from other users,\par
\tab    generally by Internet or other means.\par
\par
\tab    The included configuration of OS/8 boots to the RK8E/RK05,\par
\tab    generally mounted on RK05 unit 0.  By changing the\par
\tab    configuration of mounted drives, it is possible to boot OS/8\par
\tab    on any RK05 drive 0-3.\par
\par
\tab    Due to the manner in which the host Windows environment passes \tab    files to the SIMH simulator, it is necessary to include the\par
\tab    standard OS/8 PTR: handler.\par
\par
  \tab    Note: Due to a quirk of the PTR: handler, the very last\par
 \tab    character of the simulated PTR: character stream [contents of\par
 \tab    the PTRIN file in the SIMH_Working_Files directory] will be\par
 \tab    ignored.  As such, any file obtained from the PTR: handler\par
 \tab    will be truncated.  If the passed file is an ASCII text file, \tab    the lost character will generally be a <LF> character which\par
 \tab    can be restored by passing the file through OS/8 PIP or\par
\tab    various editing programs such as OS/8 TECO.  Certain programs\par
        which process <CR> characters and ignore <LF> characters will\par
\tab    function correctly when processing these files despite the\par
 \tab    slight defect.  Binary files with generous leader/trailer\par
 \tab    characters will not be affected by the PTR: quirk.\cf1   Is this an error in SIMH, i.e., a bug by Bob Supnik and/or other SIMH Programmers?\cf0\par
\par
\tab b) P?S/8.  This is Version 8Z of P?S/8 configured for the\par
 \tab    TC01/TC08 DECtape with up to eight logical drives [0-\cf1 7\cf0 ].  Over\par
\tab    the years, various patches have been applied to Version 8Z to \tab    correct certain known bugs.  An ECO log file is available to\par
 \tab    detail what was done since the original release of Version 8Z\par
        on 23-Feb-1988.  [Note: Future support of P?S/8 within the \tab\par
        PEPS will be taken from P?S/8 Version 9A which is currently\par
        under development.]\par
\par
\tab Note: Both OS/8 and P?S/8 are capable of booting \strike all available \tab operating systems\cf1\strike0 ?\cf0  directly using simple commands or running short \tab bootup programs which are provided in each system.  In the case \tab of the RK8E/RK05 cartridge disk device, this will allow bootup to \tab any of the four drives if a suitable \cf1 cartridge\cf0  file is mounted.\par
\par
\tab c) P?S/8 with the line printer disabled.  In earlier releases of\par
        P?S/8, many programs automatically directed output to the\par
        device 66 line printer if it was available and online.  To\par
        prevent this action, it was necessary to disable the line\par
\tab    printer either by powering down or placing the device in an\par
\tab    offline state.\page   \tab    As of the release of P?S/8 Version 8Z, most of these problems\par
        have been eliminated by implementing command-line switches in \tab    the affected programs that can force output to the console\par
 \tab    printer.  To allow testing of any remaining problems, P?S/8\par
 \tab    may be started with the device 66 printer logically disabled\par
 \tab    in the associated SIMH configuration file.  While fully\par
  \tab    supported, this system is of little interest to most PEPS\par
 \tab    users; once all known issues are resolved, this mode of\par
 \tab    operation will likely be withdrawn from future releases of the\par
 \tab    PEPS or moved to the Inactive Commands directory.\par
\par
\tab Note: Future releases of the PEPS will include additional \tab operating systems such as P?S/8 configured for the TD8E/TU56, \tab RK8E/RK05 or RX8E/RX01.  Additional OS/8 systems can be added \tab that can boot to devices other than the RK8E/RK05 such as the \tab RX8E/RX01 [or support different non-system handlers].  As the \tab Command directory window becomes more complex, it may become \tab necessary to include an internal folder for choice of operating\par
\tab system to avoid clutter; advanced users may also require \tab internal folders containing additional file development \tab icon\cf1 s\cf0 /links [other than a few currently in use in the main folder] \tab for similar reasons.  With experience, all users can determine\par
\tab which particular Windows desktop configuration serves their needs\par
\tab in an efficient manner; consider the provided Command directory\par
\tab as merely a starting point which can evolve as required.\par
\par
1.62\tab Generic file handling and related commands\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3, all generic file handling and related\par
\tab commands are located in the second row of the Command directory \tab window.\par
\par
\tab The PEPS environment includes an internal folder for files \tab currently under development which is referenced as the\par
\i\tab Current_Program_Files\i0  directory.  This is in sharp contrast with\par
\tab files stored in a more general collection of external directories \tab organized by user preference; a partial implementation of these \tab directories is provided with each release of the PEPS including \tab certain example files that are accessible with explicit commands.  \tab This file structure should serve as a guide to additional user \tab directory organization and file development.\par
\par
\tab In the course of accessing these files, it will likely become \tab necessary to interact with both the external directories and the \tab\i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory.  Commands in this section are \tab available to work with individual files in the\par
\tab\i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory as well as interact with the\par
\tab external directories using Windows Explorer techniques.\page\tab a) Edit Current Program.  The current named file is opened in a\par
 \tab    designated editor, in this case the standard Windows Notepad\par
\tab    program.  The Current Program filename is set using the \i Set\par
 \tab    Current Program\i0  command described in c) below.\par
\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves the updated state\par
\tab    of the current named file; the file is then copied to the\par
\tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used as the input to the PTR: device within the\par
\tab    SIMH simulator environment.  This allows any operating system \par
\tab    to be launched with the contents of the current named file\par
\tab    already set up.  However, it is not necessary to close the\par
\tab    editing program; if the file is saved using commands within\par
\tab    the editor, the \i Send Current Program\i0  command described in b)\par
\tab    below can be used to allow rapid editing operations on the\par
\tab    file after the designated operating system has processed the\par
 \tab    file as needed.  This is the fastest way to develop most PDP-8\par
 \tab    programs while also taking advantage of editing the file in\par
 \tab    the host Windows environment.\par
\par
\tab b) Send Current Program.  The contents of the current named file\par
        are copied to the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used as the input to the PTR:\par
        device within the SIMH environment.  The SIMH simulator is\par
 \tab    then launched in a separate window, booting to the designated\par
 \tab    operating system as defined in the corresponding underlying\par
 \tab    batch file associated with this command [\i Send Current\par
 \tab    Program.BAT\i0 ].  The Current Program filename is set using the\par
 \tab    \i Set Current Program\i0  command described in c) below.\par
\par
  \tab    Note: As released, P?S/8 with standard LPT: support is the\par
\tab    designated operating system for use with this command.  By\par
\tab    applying a small change to the \i Send Current Program.BAT\i0  file,\par
\tab    any available operating system can be chosen.  Several\par
\tab    operating system choices are already provided within the\par
\tab    batch file; all inactive choices should be preceded with a REM\par
\tab    statement to simplify further changes in the future.\par
\par
\tab c) Set Current Program.  The Current Program filename [used in a) \tab    and b) above] is set by invoking this command.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The Current Program filename is permanently stored\par
 \tab    within the PEPS package.  This command is the only method\par
        available to update the filename, which is internally stored\par
        within a key parameter file of the package; this applies even\par
        if the entire PEPS environment is transferred to a different\par
 \tab    Windows system.\par
\par
\tab    As shown in Figure 4 below, the default Current Program file\par
 \tab    name is \i HELLOWORLD.PAL\i0 .  This command allows the filename to\par
 \tab    be changed to any desired combination of alphanumeric\par
