\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab e) Edit WHATAMI.PAL.  The text file \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\tab    \\Generic\\Source Files\\WHATAMI.PAL\i0  is opened in a new window\par
        using Windows Notepad [the designated text editing program].\par
        All particulars of this command are defined within the \i Edit\par
 \tab    and Copy WHATAMI.PAL.BAT\i0  file in the \i SIMH Batch Files \par
\tab    \i0 directory.  As explained above with regard to the \i Edit Current\par
 \tab    Program \i0 command, Appendix C contains information regarding \tab\tab    alternative editing programs.\par
\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves changes made to\par
\pard  \tab    the \i WHATAMI.PAL\i0  file; the file is then copied to the \i PTRIN.TXT\par
\i0\tab    file used for input to the PTR: device within the next SIMH\par
\tab    simulator session.  This allows any operating system to be\par
\tab    launched with the contents of the \i WHATAMI.PAL\i0  already set up\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    for file capture within the designated PDP-8 operating system.\par
\pard\par
  \tab    For most editing situations it is not necessary to close the\par
 \tab    editing program each time changes are made.  By merely saving\par
\tab    the file, the \i Send WHATAMI.PAL\i0  command described in f) below\par
 \tab    can be used to allow rapidly repeating editing operations on\par
\tab    the file after the designated operating system has processed\par
\tab    the file leading to the need for additional editing.  This is\par
\tab    the fastest way to develop most PDP-8 programs while also\par
\tab    taking advantage of editing the file in the host Windows\par
\tab    environment.\page\tab f) Send WHATAMI.PAL.  The contents of the \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    \\Generic Source Files\\WHATAMI.PAL\i0  file are copied to the\par
\pard  \tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
 \tab    SIMH environment.  The SIMH simulator is then launched in a\par
 \tab    separate window, booting to the designated operating system as \tab    defined in the corresponding underlying batch file associated\par
 \tab    with this command [\i Send WHATAMI.PAL.BAT\i0 ].  Note: While P?S/8\par
 \tab    is the default operating system for this function, a one line\par
 \tab    edit to the batch file can be made to boot to OS/8 or other\par
 \tab    operating system; this option exists in all analogous batch\par
\tab    files to provide free choice of booted operating system when\par
 \tab    sending files via the PTR: mechanism.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since e) and f) above refer to a file developed within\par
 \tab    the P?S/8 environment, the associated icons for these commands\par
 \tab    are blue color.\par
\par
\tab    A P?S/8 session using \i WHATAMI.PAL\i0  passed as the input to\par
\tab    the PTR: device is shown in Figure 9.  The various steps taken\par
 \tab    are documented below:\par
\par
\tab    i)   The P?S/8 date was set to 06-Nov-2016 in a prior SIMH\par
\tab         session.  Unlike OS/8, P?S/8 retains the current date\par
\tab         permanently until changed.\par
\par
\tab         Note: P?S/8 date handling in Version 8Z will expire in\par
\tab         the year 2099.  Future releases will extend date handling\par
\tab         through 31-Dec-2411.\par
\par
\tab    ii)  The \i WHATAMI.PAL\i0  file was processed into the eight \i Tiny\par
\tab         File System\i0  [TFS] files \i WHT[1]\i0 , \i WHT[2]\i0 , \i WHT[3]\i0 , \i WHT[4]\i0 ,\par
\tab         \i WHT[5]\i0 , \i WHT[6]\i0 , \i WHT[7]\i0  and \i WHT[8]\i0  from the paper-tape\par
\tab         input stream setup in a prior SIMH session.\par
\par
\tab         Note: P?S/8 also supports extended-length files.  If this\par
\tab         method is chosen, this results in a single extended-\par
\tab         length file such as \i WHTAMI\i0 .  Since P?S/8 supports\par
\tab         multiple file structures, the user can determine which is\par
\tab         the most appropriate for a given situation.\par
\par
\tab\tab    The eight TFS files are passed to the P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  assembler\par
\tab\tab    program; command-line option switches initiate the \par
\tab         assembly process followed by an automatic chain to the\par
\tab         P\cf1 ?\cf0 S/8 \i BIN\i0  binary file loading program; the default binary\par
\tab         file\i  % \i0 is used as the output target of the assembly and\par
\tab         then the binary input file passed to \i BIN\i0 .  Since a\par
\tab         starting address is passed using \i /G=200\i0 , the program \tab\tab\tab    starts automatically after loading.\par
\par
\tab         Note: The assembler statistics show there were no\par
\tab         assembly errors.  Had there been any errors, the\par
\tab         automatic chain to \i BIN\i0  would have been prevented.\page\tab    iii) The WHATAMI.PAL program is designed to detect the\par
\tab         specific PDP-8 model the program is running on.  Since\par
\tab         the SIMH simulator is configured to support the KK8F CPU,\par
\tab         the program indicates it is running on a PDP-8/E or\par
 \tab         similar model.\cf1   Should you mention that this program will correctly identify the actual hardware PDP-8 that it is run on?\cf0\par
\par
\tab         Note: As explained in section 1.1 above, the SIMH\par
\tab         simulator provides full compatibility with the KK8F CPU.\par
\tab         As such, it is not possible for a software program to\par
 \tab         differentiate between the PDP-8/E, PDP-8/F, PDP-8/M and\par
\tab         the PDP-8/A-620 series models.\par
\par
