 Figure 11. LPTOUT.TXT file stripping session\par
\par
\tab c) Open LPT Output.  The \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file is opened in the\par
\tab    standard Windows Notepad editor.  This allows immediate\par
\tab    display of the consequences of processing such as assembling\par
\tab    the source file currently under development.\par
\par
\tab    Note: While there is generally no need to modify the\par
 \tab    \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file, editing operations are freely allowed; the\par
\tab    file may be printed if desired.  In many circumstances, the\par
\tab    font size must be reduced to obtain a complete display on the\par
\tab    Windows desktop without resorting to horizontal scrolling.\par
\tab    This typically occurs because assembly language utilities add\par
\tab    additional content at the left margin of every source file\par
\tab    line.\par
\par
\tab    While alternative editors may be useful for typical editing\par
\tab    situations, most other qualifying editing utilities may either\par
\tab    cause all open text files to share a common window [by\par
\tab    selecting one of a set of multiple tabs] or not support\par
\tab    multiple font sizes in a single session.  Unless the\par
\tab    alternate program can support these additional features, it is\par
\tab    recommended that Windows Notepad continue to be used for this\par
\tab    purpose.\par
\par
\tab d) Clear PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is emptied.  This\par
\tab    command is needed to ensure there are no residual characters\par
\tab    in the file resulting from previous SIMH sessions.  If the\par
\tab    purpose of the next SIMH session is to produce a text file\par
\tab    directed to the PTP: device, this command must precede\par
\tab    launching the SIMH simulator.\par
\par
\tab e) Strip PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is stripped of unwanted\par
\tab    artifacts; other than the change of target file from\par
 \tab    \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  to \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0 , the entire process as described in\par
\tab    b) above applies to this command.\page\tab    It has been occasionally observed that the PTPOUT.TXT file\par
\tab    will contain a trailing form-feed character after the file has\par
\tab    been stripped of all other artifacts.  The root cause of this\par
\tab    minor issue has been determined to be an obscure quirk of the\par
\tab    OS/8 PTP: handler due to an intrinsic design problem within\par
\tab    the handler.  Notably, if the identical file is processed by\par
\tab    the OS/8 LPT: handler [into the \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file] the problem\par
\tab    doesn't occur.\par
\par
\tab    Should this situation be problematic, the character can be\par
\tab    removed manually using the \i Open PTP Output\i0  command as\par
\tab    described in f) below; alternatively, the entire stripping\par
\tab    process can be repeated to eliminate the unwanted artifact.\par
\par
\tab    Due to the manner in which the OS/8 PTP: handler functions,\par
\tab    while the form-feed character is the last such character in\par
\tab    the original file, it is not actually the sentinel character\par
\tab    of the file, generally surrounded by sequences of unwanted\par
\tab    characters requiring removal at a different stage of the\par
\tab    stripping process.  Future releases of the STRIP.TEC macro may\par
\tab    be able to remove this defect in more instances than\par
\tab    presently.  [As of the current release of the stipping\par
\tab    program, this artifact is already being removed in many cases,\par
\tab    albeit apparently not in every case.]\par
\par
\tab f) Open PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is opened in the\par
\tab    standard Windows Notepad editor.  This allows immediate\par
\tab    display of the consequences of processing such as assembling\par
\tab    the source file currently under development.\par
\par
\tab    Other than the change of target file from LPTOUT.TXT to\par
\tab    PTPOUT.TXT, the entire process as described in c) above\par
\tab    applies to this command.\par
\par
\tab g) Swap PTR and PTP Files.  The contents of the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  [PTR:]\par
\tab    and \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  [PTP:] files are interchanged.  This provides\par
 \tab    file interchange useful in specific circumstances.\par
\par
\tab    For example, binary files created in P?S/8 can be made\par
\tab    available for OS/8 usage [or vice-versa] using this command.\par
\tab    This allows OS/8 users to take advantage of the unique\par
\tab    features of P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  which supports additional functionality\par
\tab    beyond OS/8 PAL8 while maintaining total PAL language\par
\tab    compatibility.\par
\par
\tab    With the availability of the P?S/8 \i PTUTIL\i0  program, text files\par
\tab    may be directly interchanged between these operating systems  \tab    without involving the host Windows environment.  Note: Other\par
 \tab    methods are available for file interchange between these two\par
\tab    operating systems.  Appendix V describes a variety of methods,\par
\tab    some of which are currently under development.\cf1   Is PTUTIL available now?\cf0\page\pard 1.65 Miscellaneous commands\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3, all miscellaneous commands are\par
\tab located in the bottom row of the Command directory window.\par
\par
\tab a) SIMH Working Files.  The \i SIMH_Working_Files\i0  \strike folder\cf1\strike0 directory\cf0  is opened\par
\tab    in a separate window.  Using Windows Explorer techniques,\par
\tab    various files may be accessed for such purposes as file\par
\tab    interchange with external sources.\par
\par
\tab    This \strike folder\cf1\strike0 directory\cf0  is also the working directory for device image\par
\tab    files.  By copying device image files here [and editing the\par
\tab    appropriate SIMH configuration files] the contents of device\par
\tab    image files can be made active in the SIMH environment.\par
\tab    Appendix Y details the important files located in this\par
 \tab    directory.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Normal actitivy of the various components of the PEPS\par
\tab    package will tend to produce temporary files in this\par
\tab    directory; in extreme cases, it may be necessary to manually \par
\tab    remove temporary files.  However, the majority of temporary\par
 \tab    filenames are internally recycled, largely avoiding any\par
        maintenance requirements.\par
\par
\tab b) Help Documents.  The \i SIMH Documents\i0  \strike folder\cf1\strike0 directory\cf0  is opened in a\par
\tab    separate window, allowing access to a variety of documentation\par
\tab    files on various PEPS-related subjects.  Since most of the\par
\tab    files are in .PDF format, a suitable program such as \i Adobe\par
\tab    Reader\i0  must be installed.  Appendix U provides additional\par
\tab    information on the available documents.\par
\par
\tab c) Inactive Programs.  The \i Inactive Programs\i0  \strike folder\cf1\strike0 directory\cf0  is opened in\par
\tab    a separate window.  Generally, icon/link files are stored here\par
\tab    for programming projects not currently under development.\par
\tab    Several of these files are provided \strike by \cf1\strike0 for \cf0 advanced users of the\par
\tab    PEPS package for the purpose of developing their own PDP-8\par
\tab    programs.  All of the files are useful as templates for\par
\tab    creating additional personal program development commands.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The provided files in the \i Inactive Programs\i0  directory\par
\tab    will not be actionable without the underlying support files.\par
\tab    In some cases, the referenced source files are proprietary and\par
\tab    are not available with the standard release of the PEPS\par
\tab    package.  However, the mechanism to exercise the icon/link\par
\tab    files should be functional because dummy files are provided in\par
\tab    lieu of the original source files.\par
\par
\tab d) SIMH Config Files.  The \i SIMH Configuration Files\i0  \strike folder\cf1\strike0 directory\cf0  is\par
\tab    opened in a separate window.  Within this file are the\par
\tab    configuration files used with each variant operating system\par
\tab    used with the SIMH simulator.  Changes made to these files can\par
\tab    greatly affect the way the corresponding operating systems\par
 \tab    function.\page References.\par
\par
 1. The \i Poly Question Society\i0  [abbreviated wherever possible as P?S or\par
    PQS if only a limited character set is available and pronounced as\par
    if spelled \ul pee-cue-ess\ulnone ] is an informal group of questioners\par
    concerned with a multitude of subjects.  In general, the members\par
    of the P?S [whether alive, dead or imaginary] met at an\par
    institution of higher learning known as the \i Polytechnic Institute\par
    of Brooklyn \i0 or more informally "Brookyn Poly" [now a division of\par
    \i New York University\i0 ].  Nearly all P?S members are computer\par
    programmers known for exceptional works designed for various\par
    computer architectures, especially the DEC PDP-8.\par
\par
    The P?S is an outgrowth of a preexisting organization known as the\par
    \i William Claude Fields Memorial Programming Group\i0  [WCFMPG] [also\par
    known as \i Friends of the Boston Massacre\i0 ].  The work of the WCFMPG\par
    is legendary in the realm of PDP-8 programming; all original\par
    members were responsible for either starting or creating various\par
    software projects for DEC computers, especially the PDP-8.  All\par
    members of the WCFMPG were employees of DEC at some point in their\par
    careers; however, much of their work on PDP-8 systems [and other\par
    computers made by other companies] predates their employment at\par
    Digital.\cf1   So you weren't in the WCFMPG, but were in the P?S.  Right (since you never worked for DEC)?\cf0\par
\par
 2. OS/8 is the princip\strike le\cf1\strike0 al\cf0  marketing name of an operating system that\par
    can run on most PDP-8 models [or in some instances a specific\par
    variant name is used because of the need to support slightly\par
    different and likely incompatible models which cannot run the main\par
    system components].  The group of name variant systems constitues\par
    a confusing mess because some of these variations were merely\par
    created for the purposes of highly dubious marketing ploys; only\par
    an expert can tell the difference between a variant with little or\par
    no technical purpose over OS/8 Version 3D [also known as 3Q] and\par
    another seemingly slightly different system that can only be used\par
    with DECmates albeit not necessarily completely functional].  In\par
    general it is prudent to avoid these newer variants when using the\par
    SIMH simulator due to the likelihood of incompatibility.\par
\par
    Certain specific programs [taken from newer release variants\par
    mentioned above] provide good functionality when used in OS/8 V3D.\par
    As such, the image copy of OS/8 provided in the PEPS package\par
    contains several notable programs that correct bugs and quirks in\par
    the original\cf1 ly\cf0  released versions associated with OS/8 V3D.\par
\par
    OS/8 was originally created as a quick-and-dirty hack pulled\par
    off over a single weekend intended as a demo program to be shown\par
    to DEC management the next Monday morning.  While there was every\par
    intention to write a second system with more reasoned design\par
    internals, foolishly, management insisted all further changes be\par
    made to the demo version instead of a complete rewrite.  As a\par
    direct result, many serious problems became institutionalized\par
    into the design that cannot be corrected.\page     The original demo [and some of the follow\cf1 -\cf0 on work] was done by\par
    Richard Lary, who also created most of the R-L Monitor System\par
    [also known later as \i MS/8\i0 ] along with the rest of the members of\par
    the WCFMPG.  The R-L Monitor System was the first PDP-8 operating\par
    system ever used by most of the members of the P?S.\par
\par
    Part of the reason the deficient demo was created is \cf1 because? \cf0 the R-L\par
    Monitor System was shown to DEC management personnel, all of whom\par
    rejected it essentially because they had no idea what it was or\par
    what it did; the demo was designed to show how a computer user\par
    could approach the PDP-8 in a manner vaguely similar to the way\par
    this was done on other DEC computers available at the time.  As\par
    such, the managers obtained a completely superficial understanding\par
    of the demo [which for them was the most they could muster\par
    considering their minimal understanding of the other operating\par
    systems mentioned above that were in place].\par
\par
    It is noteworthy that all of the other members of the WCFMPG\par
    refused to work on the demo because of its flimsy nature.  Later,\par
    some other members of the P?S were hired by DEC to work on OS/8\par
    along with a few unrelated staffers of varying abilities.  While\par
    this mixed group did develop the demo into something somewhat\par
    better, the inherent internal limitations would never be overcome.\par
\par
    Using OS/8 today, whether on real-world hardware or within the\par
    PEPS environment, requires certain quirky skills on the part of\par
    the user such as working in a mythical year environment sometime\par
    prior to 01-Jan-2002.  Unfortunately, due to limitations in the\par
    file system, it is not possible to determine the proper year of\par
    any particular file because there is an inherent anomaly regarding\par
    which group of eight years in the range of 1970-1977, 1978-1985,\par
    1986-1993 or 1994-2001 is the true file origination date.\cf1   So RL only had/used 3 bits for the year?\cf0\par
\par
    In specific instances, particular users know the proper year group\par
    for certain files from personal experience, but inevitably, any\par
    collection of files will become commingled with files from one of\par
    the other three possible date groups; worse still, program files\par
    created today must have mythical dates.  Newer programmers must\par
    keep track of the real-world date offset on a file by file basis.\par
    Because of this serious anomaly, coupled with  the advantages\par
    offered by advanced features of the PEPS package, it is strongly\par
    recommended that users embrace host editing of source files, where\par
    more descriptive filenames are possible with accurate file dates.\par
\par
    Certain intrepid users are known to manually edit in mythical date\par
    modifications in the output files of OS/8 directory listings to\par
    maintain fantasy records that may contain more information than\par
    OS/8 can actually provide.  However, due to Y2K considerations,\par
    this practice has an upper limit of 31-Dec-2069.\page  3. P?S/8 is a replacement system for the R-L Monitor system.  It \par
    includes thousands of improvements borne of all-new work carried\par
    out over the course of decades.  Despite this, the basic system is\par
    as low-overhead [or in some minor ways actually lower overhead] as\par
    is possible for a small-scale PDP-8 system as compared to the R-L\par
    Monitor System.  On many specific hardware configurations, P?S/8\par
    runs palpably faster than OS/8 performing comparable tasks.\par
\par
   Certain specific concepts unique to the R-L Monitor System [and not\par
   translatable to OS/8] have either been faithfully implemented in\par
   P?S/8, or in some cases, actually improved.\par
\par
   P?S/8 is still being developed.  While the P?S/8 release included\par
   in the PEPS package is Version 8Z first released 23-Feb-1988,\par
   various improvements and bug fixes have been applied to the system\par
   image included in the PEPS release.\line\line    As soon as P?S/8 Version 9A is completed, it will replace the\par
   present Version 8Z release and be included in future PEPS packages.\line\line    P?S/8 runs on all of the same PDP-8 models that OS/8 [and its\par
   variants] runs on, however, one P?S/8 release covers all models,\par
   differing only in terms of specific device handlers and related\par
   utilities where applicable.  It is noteworthy that a P?S/8 system\par
   based on RX01 diskette media can be booted to all models from the\par
   original "straight" PDP-8 through the DECmate II.  \cf1 (Not the DECmate III+?)  \cf0 By way of\par
   comparison, three different OS/8 variants are required to support\par
   the same models due to insurmountable incompatibilities inherent in\par
   OS/8 internal design [and at least four were marketed for the\par
   purpose].\par
\par
   A minor patch has to be applied to the supplied P?S/8 release for\par
   the TC01/TC08 DECtape controller due to SIMH incompatibility with\par
   the real-world hardware.  Documentation is available explaining the\par
   minor change which is reversible if moving the system image to an\par
   actual DECtape for use on real-world systems.  Various programs\par
   exist to move DECtape images between actual DECtapes and the \par
   SIMH-compatible files used in PEPS.  Appendix D provides links to\par
   appropriate utilities for this and related purposes.\par
\par
4. BIN format is the standard binary loading format defined in the\par
   paper-tape software system.  On occasion, a BIN format file must be\par
   loaded into memory in the SIMH simulated environment.  Both the\par
   OS/8 and P?S/8 operating systems support utilities to process BIN\par
   format data passed to the SIMH environment by setting up the\par
   \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file with the intended binary contents.  When files are\par
   moved from the SIMH environment to the host Windows system, the\par
   \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is used.\page\par
